WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.295
Lesson 3.7.

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:06.020
CDP stablecoins: minting your own dollars.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.075
By the end, you'll be able to mint a

00:00:08.075 --> 00:00:11.241
stablecoin against your own collateral, the way your own

00:00:11.241 --> 00:00:13.685
bank would, and manage that position safely.

00:00:14.985 --> 00:00:16.275
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.275 --> 00:00:18.346
What a C.D.P. actually is, and

00:00:18.346 --> 00:00:20.250
what you owe back on it.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.490
The minimum collateral ratio, the line that decides liquidation.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.827
How the stablecoin's peg actually holds,

00:00:26.827 --> 00:00:30.035
through four different mechanisms working together.

00:00:30.035 --> 00:00:31.522
How this differs from simply

00:00:31.522 --> 00:00:33.360
borrowing from a lending pool.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.468
And a full worked example with real

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:38.220
numbers, minting half of the available maximum.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.275
What a CDP actually is.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:46.850
Here's a C.D.P., a collateralised debt position, in full.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:48.795
You lock collateral, an asset

00:00:48.795 --> 00:00:51.105
like E.T.H., into the position.

00:00:51.105 --> 00:00:54.344
Against that locked collateral, you mint a stablecoin,

00:00:54.344 --> 00:00:57.053
meaning the protocol creates new units of it,

00:00:57.053 --> 00:01:00.180
specifically for you, backed by what you locked.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.018
You owe that stablecoin back eventually, plus

00:01:03.018 --> 00:01:05.362
a stability fee, which is really just

00:01:05.362 --> 00:01:08.015
interest, charged on the amount you minted.

00:01:09.315 --> 00:01:11.581
Here's the full lifecycle, from opening

00:01:11.581 --> 00:01:13.410
the position to closing it.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:17.447
You lock collateral into the C.D.P. You mint stablecoins

00:01:17.447 --> 00:01:21.360
against it, up to some maximum the protocol allows.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.633
You use those stablecoins for whatever you actually need

00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:27.964
them for, while your collateral keeps earning nothing, or

00:01:27.964 --> 00:01:31.365
in some designs, still earning its own separate yield.

00:01:31.365 --> 00:01:34.588
And eventually, you repay the minted amount plus the

00:01:34.588 --> 00:01:38.420
accrued stability fee, to unlock your original collateral back.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:40.810
Quick check.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.085
What do you actually owe on a C.D.P.?

00:01:47.505 --> 00:01:50.917
The answer: the stablecoin you minted, plus the accrued

00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:54.450
stability fee, the interest charged on that minted amount.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.530
Here's what a C.D.P. is actually used for

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.875
in practice, beyond just the mechanism itself.

00:02:01.875 --> 00:02:05.379
Getting liquidity without selling the underlying asset at

00:02:05.379 --> 00:02:08.639
all, keeping your original position fully intact.

00:02:08.639 --> 00:02:11.581
Funding a liquidity ladder, minting in stages as

00:02:11.581 --> 00:02:14.163
you need cash rather than all at once.

00:02:14.163 --> 00:02:17.343
Or building a yield-covered credit line, where yield

00:02:17.343 --> 00:02:20.223
from elsewhere covers the ongoing stability fee.

00:02:21.523 --> 00:02:23.248
The minimum collateral ratio.

00:02:24.548 --> 00:02:28.012
Here's what this ratio actually measures, and why it's

00:02:28.012 --> 00:02:31.162
the single number that matters most on any C.D.P.

00:02:31.162 --> 00:02:34.815
It's your collateral's current value, divided by how much

00:02:34.815 --> 00:02:39.083
stablecoin you've minted against it, expressed as a percentage.

00:02:39.083 --> 00:02:42.313
A common minimum might be one hundred fifty percent.

00:02:42.313 --> 00:02:44.282
As long as your ratio stays above

00:02:44.282 --> 00:02:46.608
that minimum, the position is safe.

00:02:46.608 --> 00:02:48.774
The moment it falls below, through a

00:02:48.774 --> 00:02:51.878
collateral price drop, the position gets liquidated,

00:02:51.878 --> 00:02:55.098
automatically, to protect the stablecoin's own backing.

00:02:56.398 --> 00:02:59.197
Worth being precise about the actual relationship

00:02:59.197 --> 00:03:01.437
this creates, between how much you mint

00:03:01.437 --> 00:03:03.808
and how much room you leave yourself.

00:03:03.808 --> 00:03:06.830
Minting the maximum the protocol allows puts you

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:10.098
exactly at the minimum ratio, meaning any collateral

00:03:10.098 --> 00:03:13.543
price drop at all triggers liquidation immediately.

00:03:13.543 --> 00:03:16.885
Minting less than the maximum is what actually buys

00:03:16.885 --> 00:03:19.328
you room to survive a real price move.

00:03:20.628 --> 00:03:22.503
How much you mint decides your cushion.

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:27.973
Here's that exact relationship, plotted across five different

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:31.067
mint amounts, all against the same ten E.T.H.

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:34.058
of collateral at three thousand dollars.

00:03:34.058 --> 00:03:36.732
Minting five thousand dollars gives you a

00:03:36.732 --> 00:03:39.661
liquidation price of just seven hundred fifty

00:03:39.661 --> 00:03:42.953
dollars, a full seventy-five percent drop away.

00:03:42.953 --> 00:03:45.850
Minting ten thousand, the amount from our worked

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:49.638
example, gives fifteen hundred dollars, fifty percent away.

00:03:49.638 --> 00:03:52.133
Minting eighteen thousand pushes it up to

00:03:52.133 --> 00:03:55.523
twenty-seven hundred dollars, only ten percent away.

00:03:55.523 --> 00:03:58.178
And minting the full twenty thousand dollar

00:03:58.178 --> 00:04:00.834
maximum pushes your liquidation price up to

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:03.851
exactly three thousand dollars, the current price

00:04:03.851 --> 00:04:05.963
itself, with zero room left at all.

00:04:07.263 --> 00:04:09.712
Notice what happens right at the far end of

00:04:09.712 --> 00:04:13.073
that curve, at the full twenty thousand dollar maximum.

00:04:13.073 --> 00:04:15.673
The liquidation price doesn't just get close to

00:04:15.673 --> 00:04:18.573
the current price; it becomes the current price.

00:04:18.573 --> 00:04:20.922
There's no buffer left at all, no room

00:04:20.922 --> 00:04:24.293
for even the smallest, most ordinary market wobble.

00:04:24.293 --> 00:04:27.159
That's the cliff edge from a moment ago, expressed

00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:29.688
as an actual number instead of a description.

00:04:30.989 --> 00:04:32.079
Quick check.

00:04:32.079 --> 00:04:35.208
On that same ten E.T.H. of collateral, roughly

00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:38.604
what's your liquidation price if you mint eighteen

00:04:38.604 --> 00:04:41.334
thousand dollars instead of ten thousand?

00:04:45.754 --> 00:04:49.119
The answer: about twenty-seven hundred dollars, only a

00:04:49.119 --> 00:04:51.995
ten percent drop away, since minting closer to

00:04:51.995 --> 00:04:54.014
the maximum leaves far less room.

00:04:55.314 --> 00:04:56.789
How the peg actually holds.

00:04:58.089 --> 00:05:00.741
Here's all four mechanisms that actually keep

00:05:00.741 --> 00:05:03.162
this stablecoin near one dollar, since no

00:05:03.162 --> 00:05:05.364
single one of them does it alone.

00:05:05.364 --> 00:05:09.367
Over-collateralisation means there's always more locked value

00:05:09.367 --> 00:05:13.024
backing the stablecoin than stablecoin in existence.

00:05:13.024 --> 00:05:16.639
Liquidations remove undercollateralised positions before

00:05:16.639 --> 00:05:18.969
they become a genuine shortfall.

00:05:18.969 --> 00:05:22.258
Interest rates, the stability fee, can be adjusted to

00:05:22.258 --> 00:05:26.029
change how attractive minting versus holding actually is.

00:05:26.029 --> 00:05:28.662
And a peg stability module lets holders

00:05:28.662 --> 00:05:31.689
swap the stablecoin one-to-one, minus a small

00:05:31.689 --> 00:05:34.059
fee, directly for other stablecoins.

00:05:35.359 --> 00:05:38.390
Here's the actual difference between a C.D.P. and

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.360
simply borrowing from a lending pool, since they

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.994
can look similar but work quite differently underneath.

00:05:44.994 --> 00:05:48.226
Borrowing from a pool means you're borrowing someone

00:05:48.226 --> 00:05:50.909
else's actual deposit, and the rate you pay

00:05:50.909 --> 00:05:53.593
is set by that pool's utilisation, how much

00:05:53.593 --> 00:05:55.844
of the pool is already lent out.

00:05:55.844 --> 00:05:59.796
A C.D.P. instead creates new money directly, and the

00:05:59.796 --> 00:06:03.375
rate you pay is set by protocol governance, not

00:06:03.375 --> 00:06:06.134
by any deposit or utilisation at all.

00:06:07.434 --> 00:06:10.560
Worth being precise about why that governance-set

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.248
rate actually matters to you directly, not

00:06:13.248 --> 00:06:15.049
just as a design detail.

00:06:15.049 --> 00:06:17.925
Because it isn't tied to utilisation, it can move

00:06:17.925 --> 00:06:20.513
for reasons that have nothing to do with how

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:24.839
much you're personally borrowing, purely as a peg-management decision.

00:06:24.839 --> 00:06:27.594
Always compare it against what a lending pool

00:06:27.594 --> 00:06:30.648
would currently charge for the same amount, before

00:06:30.648 --> 00:06:32.983
assuming the CDP is the cheaper choice.

00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:35.373
Quick check.

00:06:35.373 --> 00:06:37.898
What does a peg stability module actually do?

00:06:42.318 --> 00:06:45.840
The answer: it swaps the stablecoin one-to-one, minus a

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.803
small fee, with other stablecoins, directly supporting the peg.

00:06:51.103 --> 00:06:52.108
Worked example.

00:06:53.408 --> 00:06:55.563
Here's the setup, step by step.

00:06:55.563 --> 00:06:58.414
Ten E.T.H. at three thousand dollars each

00:06:58.414 --> 00:07:01.973
is thirty thousand dollars of collateral, total.

00:07:01.973 --> 00:07:05.220
At a one hundred fifty percent minimum ratio, the

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.051
absolute maximum you could mint is thirty thousand divided

00:07:09.051 --> 00:07:12.143
by one point five: twenty thousand dollars.

00:07:12.143 --> 00:07:14.623
Instead of minting that maximum, mint just

00:07:14.623 --> 00:07:17.333
ten thousand dollars, half of what's available.

00:07:18.633 --> 00:07:22.290
Here's what that more conservative choice actually buys you,

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.528
in real numbers, compared to minting the maximum.

00:07:25.528 --> 00:07:28.481
Minting the full twenty thousand puts you exactly

00:07:28.481 --> 00:07:31.611
at the one hundred fifty percent minimum, liquidated

00:07:31.611 --> 00:07:33.388
by any price drop at all.

00:07:33.388 --> 00:07:36.258
Minting ten thousand instead gives you a three

00:07:36.258 --> 00:07:39.799
hundred percent ratio, and pushes your actual liquidation

00:07:39.799 --> 00:07:42.363
price all the way down to fifteen hundred

00:07:42.363 --> 00:07:45.538
dollars, a fifty percent collateral price drop away.

00:07:46.838 --> 00:07:49.592
And here's the ongoing cost of holding that

00:07:49.592 --> 00:07:53.221
more conservative position open, using this program's own

00:07:53.221 --> 00:07:56.713
calculator with a six percent annual stability fee.

00:07:56.713 --> 00:07:59.952
Ten thousand dollars minted, at six percent, comes

00:07:59.952 --> 00:08:02.682
to six hundred dollars a year in stability

00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:05.413
fee, the price of that extra safety margin.

00:08:06.713 --> 00:08:10.365
Worth being precise about the actual lesson this comparison

00:08:10.365 --> 00:08:13.408
proves, since it's easy to treat the maximum mint

00:08:13.408 --> 00:08:16.143
as a target rather than a warning label.

00:08:16.143 --> 00:08:19.293
The protocol's maximum isn't a recommendation; it's

00:08:19.293 --> 00:08:21.595
simply the point at which any further

00:08:21.595 --> 00:08:24.318
minting would put you at immediate risk.

00:08:24.318 --> 00:08:26.696
Operators treat that maximum as a cliff

00:08:26.696 --> 00:08:28.659
edge to stay well back from, not

00:08:28.659 --> 00:08:29.848
a number to aim for.

00:08:31.148 --> 00:08:32.238
Quick check.

00:08:32.238 --> 00:08:34.835
With forty thousand dollars of collateral, at

00:08:34.835 --> 00:08:37.263
a one hundred fifty percent minimum ratio,

00:08:37.263 --> 00:08:39.183
what's the maximum you could mint?

00:08:43.603 --> 00:08:47.677
The answer: about twenty-six thousand six hundred sixty-seven

00:08:47.677 --> 00:08:50.963
dollars, forty thousand divided by one point five.

00:08:52.263 --> 00:08:53.493
Closing the position.

00:08:54.793 --> 00:08:57.628
Here's how you actually close a C.D.P., step

00:08:57.628 --> 00:09:00.448
by step, once you're ready to unwind it.

00:09:00.448 --> 00:09:03.293
Repay the stablecoin you originally minted.

00:09:03.293 --> 00:09:06.504
Repay the stability fee that's accrued since you opened

00:09:06.504 --> 00:09:09.728
it, which grows the longer the position stays open.

00:09:09.728 --> 00:09:12.441
Once both are repaid in full, your original

00:09:12.441 --> 00:09:15.648
collateral unlocks, and is returned to you directly.

00:09:16.948 --> 00:09:19.855
Here's why the moment you choose to close actually

00:09:19.855 --> 00:09:22.663
matters, not just the act of closing itself.

00:09:22.663 --> 00:09:25.117
If your collateral's price has risen since

00:09:25.117 --> 00:09:27.400
you opened the position, your ratio has

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.740
already improved on its own, purely from

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:32.723
that price move, before you've repaid anything.

00:09:32.723 --> 00:09:35.045
If it's fallen instead, your ratio has

00:09:35.045 --> 00:09:37.963
already worsened, and closing sooner, before the

00:09:37.963 --> 00:09:40.702
fee accrues further, becomes the safer choice.

00:09:42.002 --> 00:09:43.462
Here's your checklist.

00:09:43.462 --> 00:09:45.607
Write it down before you mint anything.

00:09:45.607 --> 00:09:48.309
Set a collateral ratio target well above the

00:09:48.309 --> 00:09:51.490
minimum, something like two hundred fifty percent or

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:54.852
higher, along with specific action levels below it.

00:09:54.852 --> 00:09:58.025
Compare the stability fee against what a lending pool

00:09:58.025 --> 00:10:01.381
would actually charge you to borrow the same amount.

00:10:01.381 --> 00:10:04.175
And understand the peg mechanism, the PSM

00:10:04.175 --> 00:10:06.903
and rate adjustments, plus your own exit

00:10:06.903 --> 00:10:09.232
plan if the stablecoin ever depegs.

00:10:10.532 --> 00:10:11.777
Let's recap.

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:15.301
A C.D.P. lets you mint a stablecoin against locked

00:10:15.301 --> 00:10:18.987
collateral; you owe it back plus a stability fee.

00:10:18.987 --> 00:10:21.126
The minimum collateral ratio is the

00:10:21.126 --> 00:10:24.337
line: below it, you're liquidated, automatically.

00:10:24.337 --> 00:10:27.653
The peg holds through four mechanisms together:

00:10:27.653 --> 00:10:31.752
over-collateralisation, liquidations, rates, and a PSM.

00:10:31.752 --> 00:10:34.936
A C.D.P. creates new money, unlike borrowing

00:10:34.936 --> 00:10:37.712
someone else's deposit from a pool.

00:10:37.712 --> 00:10:39.950
And the maximum mint is a cliff edge,

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.482
not a target, worth staying well back from.

00:10:43.782 --> 00:10:46.581
Set your collateral ratio target well above the

00:10:46.581 --> 00:10:49.964
minimum, compare the stability fee to lending-pool rates,

00:10:49.964 --> 00:10:52.422
and know your exit if the peg breaks.

00:10:52.422 --> 00:10:54.347
That's the end of Module 3.

00:10:54.347 --> 00:10:55.832
Next up, Module 4.
